  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 250 PM CDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nThe first day of Fall comes in with classic Fall weather. Highs\nwill be in the upper 60s and lower 70s across the area with\nmostly cloudy skies and a few peeks of sun through the\nafternoon. The 500-hPa pattern has taken an interesting\nevolution over the past few hours. Mid-level troughing has dug\ninto the West Coast while ridging from Texas to the central \nRockies has shifted slightly east and ridging over the Great \nLakes has remained fairly stagnant. This has created a ridge \nover ridge setup, however, there is a shortwave embedded between\nthem with an associated weak jet maxima placed over the MO \nRiver Valley. At the surface, a broad surface high has been \ngradually shifting east across Ontario and Quebec, decreasing \nits influence across the lower MO River Valley. An inversion \naround 850-hPa has trapped a layer of moisture near the surface \nand resulted in the cloud cover that has been persistent over \nthe past couple of days. Model soundings depict weak instability\nbeneath the inversion, which has provided enough support to \ninduce some pockets of drizzle/light rain. This will continue \nthrough the afternoon, gradually shifting east with time.\n\nThe ridge over the Great Lakes and the shortwave will move to\nthe east over the next day or so allowing the southern ridge to\ntake hold over the area. With the shortwave out of the area,\ndrier mid-level air will advect into the area and skies will \nbe able to clear through the day tomorrow. Temperatures will \nremain in the low-to-mid 70s, with dew points in the upper 50s,\ncreating a pleasant Fall day. This pattern remains through the \nend of the week.\n\nSeveral shortwaves are expected to move through the flow \ninducing showers, however, precipitation should remain to the \nnorth and west of the area through Thursday. On Friday, a more \nvigorous shortwave is progged to move through the flow and shift\nthe ridge to the east, allowing showers to move into the area \nfrom northwest to southeast through the day on Friday. Several \nmore impulses bring shower chances (30-50%) to the area through \nthe weekend. Instability looks to be minimal with these \nsystems, so even as much as thunder may be hard to come by.\n\nLong range deterministic and ensemble models are in fairly good\nagreement for mid-level ridging to build back into the Central\nCONUS by early next week. This would likely allow temperatures\nto climb back into the low-to-mid 80s.",
